From c7e4e8f6c2360639054fec3e2f8c3ff771a423cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-v7 <v7@localhost>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 19:44:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] v7: post-allocation F64 producer->consumer register
 coalescing

A single-use F64 value feeding a binary FP op (a load into a sub/mul/add, or a
chain link) is now reassigned to its consumer's destination register, so the
load lands directly in the op's register and the reg-to-reg copy that preceded
every FP operation disappears. Candidates are the LHS operand only (the emitter
loads the LHS into the destination; coalescing the RHS there would let the LHS
load clobber it - observed 'subsd %xmm4,%xmm4'=0 miscompile during
development), processed in reverse program order so an entire dependency chain
folds onto the tail register, with explicit interval-conflict checks (adjacent
producer/consumer may share: read precedes write within one instruction).

The linear scan is untouched; only the final assignment map changes.

Measured (min-of-5, pinned, vs gcc -O2):
  nbody        4.25x -> 3.48x  (advance copies 34 -> 11)
  mandelbrot   2.12x -> 1.89x
  struct_copy  6.65x -> 6.27x

Correctness: regression 127/127, correctness 50/50, intrinsics 3/3, 11
benchmarks bit-exact vs gcc, reduction difftests 0..300 x {AVX2,SSE2}, zlib-ng
adler32 correct.
